The '''late colonial history of Burawa''' encompasses the period from approximately 1620, following the settlement of the [[War of the Mesogean]], to the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709. Over nearly a century, the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] consolidated its colonial possessions, experienced a cultural flowering influenced by the [[Mesogean Renaissance]], and witnessed the emergence of intellectual currents that would later be recognised as precursors to the [[Enlightenment|era of enlightenment]]. The period concluded with the death of [[Andrias III]] without a clear heir, precipitating a succession crisis that permanently divided the Burawi state into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] (east) and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] (west).

Three kings governed during the period, all of the [[Kadim-Galwa]] cadet branch: '''[[Nuur III]]''' (r. 1608–1655), who oversaw the post-war retrenchment; '''[[Faarax IV]]''' (r. 1655–1690), whose long reign was marked by stability and cultural achievement; and '''[[Andrias III]]''' (r. 1690–1709), a capable ruler whose personal tragedy — infertility and sudden death without an heir — shattered the Galwa monopoly and divided the kingdom.

== Consolidation under Nuur III (1620–1655) ==

The accession of [[Nuur III]] in 1608, following the civil war that deposed the [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] line, inaugurated a century of continuous [[Kadim-Galwa]] rule. The new king inherited a kingdom that had lost its strategic foothold in the [[Mesogean Sea]] and its premier naval fleet at the [[Battle of Reige]] (1605). The immediate task was retrenchment: consolidating what remained and reorienting the kingdom's strategic posture toward the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and the colonial sphere.

=== Colonial reorganisation ===

The post-war settlement required a thorough reorganisation of colonial administration. The [[War of the Mesogean]] had exposed the weaknesses of the existing structure: colonial governors had exercised broad discretionary authority during the conflict, and communication between the metropole and the overseas territories had proven inadequate for coordinated defence.

Nuur III's government undertook a systematic centralisation of colonial governance. The [[Burawi Cusub|colonial territories]] in [[Abyala]] were placed under tighter metropolitan control. [[Lacashe]], the oldest and most valuable colony, was removed from the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] system entirely and placed under direct royal administration. The port city's plantation economy, slave trade, and gold exports were too valuable to be entrusted to the traditional landed aristocracy. Instead, a new class of royal administrators — drawn from the merchant class and the lesser nobility — was appointed to govern the colony.

[[Bindorama]], the middle province, was assigned a different function. The province's mountainous terrain and proximity to the frontier made it less suited to plantation agriculture, and the crown used it as a destination for [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] families whose power in the peninsula was being systematically reduced. Land grants in Bindorama offered younger sons and displaced clans an opportunity to rebuild their fortunes overseas, while simultaneously draining the peninsula of Hoga power. The [[Dakari people|Dakari]] migrants from northern Burawa, attracted by the same land grants, established a distinct cultural presence in the province's mountain valleys.

=== Hoga marginalisation ===

The marginalisation of the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy was a defining feature of the late colonial period. The Hoga families had been the backbone of the medieval Burawi state, providing military levies and governing the frontier territories. By the seventeenth century, however, the crown's revenue increasingly derived from colonial trade and customs duties rather than agricultural tribute. The economic basis of Hoga power was eroding, and the crown accelerated the process.

Colonial appointments were denied to the major Hoga houses. Military commands in the colonies were given to professional officers rather than Hoga levies. The [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts, which controlled the slave trade, were garrisoned by royal troops and administered by crown officers. The Hoga were progressively reduced to a purely peninsular aristocracy, their wealth dependent on agricultural estates whose relative value was declining.

The policy was deliberate. The [[Kadim-Galwa]] branch, which had come to power with Hoga support in 1608, was determined to prevent the Hoga from becoming a rival power centre. By channeling Hoga energy into colonial settlement — particularly in Bindorama — the crown both weakened the peninsular aristocracy and strengthened the colonial project.

== Stability and flowering under Faarax IV (1655–1690) ==

[[Faarax IV]], who acceded in 1655, inherited a consolidated kingdom and ruled over a period of stability and cultural achievement. His reign, the longest of the late colonial period, saw the resolution of external conflicts, the development of new naval technologies, and the flourishing of [[Mesogean Renaissance]] influence in Burawi intellectual and artistic life.

=== The Treaty of Ibadan ===

{{main|Treaty of Ibadan}}

The [[Treaty of Ibadan]] (ratified 1653, implemented under Faarax IV's early reign) resolved a colonial dispute that had festered since the [[War of the Mesogean]]. During the war, [[Breisland]] — a growing [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] colonial power — had dispatched a fleet around [[North Kaftia]] and established a trading outpost in the northern [[Ayorin]] region, near the [[Tamazgha]] desert. Through a combination of diplomacy and military pressure, Breisland had cultivated factions among the Ayori elite and engineered a conflict that transformed the region into a Breisland puppet vassal. Within two decades, the vassal state had been formally incorporated as a Breisland colony.

The treaty recognised Breisland's sovereignty over northern Ayorin in exchange for Breisland's withdrawal from other Burawi spheres and favourable trade terms. The cession represented a significant territorial loss, but it resolved a dispute that had threatened to draw Burawa into a wider conflict with multiple Illypnian powers. The kingdom's strategic focus remained on the Aethiopean route and the Abyalan colonies; the Ayorin interior had always been a secondary concern.

=== Volisania relations: conflict and rapprochement ===

Relations with the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] underwent a significant evolution during Faarax IV's reign. The two powers had emerged from the [[War of the Mesogean]] as rivals, but the changing dynamics of colonial competition produced a gradual rapprochement.

In the 1660s, a [[Aethiopean War]] erupted when Volisanian naval forces impressed Burawi sailors to serve in Volisania's colonial conflicts, particularly against [[Zong]] colonial possessions in the New World. Burawa viewed the impressment as a violation of sovereignty and a renewal of Volisanian aggression. A brief naval conflict followed, fought primarily in [[Abyala]]n waters and along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] trade routes. The war was inconclusive, ending with a negotiated settlement that reaffirmed Burawi neutrality in Volisania's colonial conflicts.

By the 1670s and 1680s, however, the strategic context had shifted. The proliferation of [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] colonial powers — [[Echia]], [[Merica]], [[Aetolia]], and others — and the growing presence of [[Fosia]]n colonists on the western coast of the New World created competitive pressures that favoured Burawi-Volisanian cooperation. Both kingdoms recognised that their historic rivalry benefited third parties at their mutual expense.

The two powers cooperated in a [[Abyalan Anti-Piracy Campaign]] against indigenous pirates who had established themselves along the Abyalan coast, preying on shipping from both colonial spheres. The operation, conducted in the 1680s, marked the formal end of Burawi-Volisanian hostilities and the beginning of a strategic alignment that would persist into the eighteenth century.

=== Mesogean Renaissance influence ===

The wealth generated by colonial trade — gold from [[Lacashe]], slave trade revenues, and the broader triangle trade connecting [[Kaftia]], [[Abyala]], and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world — funded a cultural flowering that historians have termed the Burawi phase of the [[Mesogean Renaissance]]. Illypnian artisans, scholars, and architects found patronage in [[Samara]] and [[Mazar]]; Burawi nobles and merchants commissioned works that blended Mesogean forms with Kaftian traditions.

The most conspicuous achievement of the period was the [[Cathedral of Weli Markos]], constructed in the city of [[Weli Markos]] between 1662 and 1678. The cathedral represented the apex of [[Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis]] in ecclesiastical architecture: a basilica plan adapted to the Burawi climate, with a vast interior nave, colonnades of local stone, and a façade incorporating both Mesogean classical elements and indigenous Kaftian decorative motifs. The cathedral became the seat of the [[Burawi Catholic Church|colonial bishopric]] and the site of state ceremonies.

=== The Great Testimony ===

{{main|Great Testimony}}

The '''[[Great Testimony]]''' (Burawi: ''Qiritaalka Weyn''), completed in 1685, was a comprehensive historical work that compiled the history of Burawa from its founding to the late seventeenth century. Commissioned by Faarax IV and compiled by a team of royal chroniclers, Church scholars, and oral historians, the Testimony synthesised written records, archival documents, and the oral traditions of the [[gewel]] bardic caste. The work served both as a legitimising chronicle of the [[Kadim]] dynasty and as an attempt to preserve traditions that were being lost as the gewel were marginalised and the northern territories assimilated into Burawi culture.

=== Naval technology ===

The [[War of the Mesogean]] had exposed the limitations of Burawi naval architecture. The fleet that sailed to [[Reige]] in 1605 was composed of vessels designed for coastal defence and colonial logistics; the long trans-Mesogean crossing and the engagement with the modernised Volisanian navy had revealed their weaknesses.

Under Faarax IV, the royal shipyards at [[Badalga]] developed the [[Badalga-class]] vessel, optimised for the Atlantic route to [[Abyala]]. The [[Badalga-class]] ships were faster, more manoeuvrable, and capable of carrying larger cargoes — essential for the triangle trade that had become the economic foundation of the kingdom. The technological advance reinforced Burawa's position in the Aethiopean and secured the colonial supply lines against both pirates and rival powers.

== Andrias III and the gathering crisis (1690–1709) ==

[[Andrias III]], who acceded in 1690, was a capable administrator and a patron of the arts and sciences. His reign, however, was overshadowed by a personal tragedy that would ultimately destroy the kingdom he governed: the king was infertile, likely as a consequence of syphilis contracted in his youth. Despite multiple marriages, he produced no heir. The succession question, obvious to all observers, cast a shadow over the final two decades of the unified Burawi state.

=== Church anti-slavery position ===

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]], which had supported the institution of slavery since the early colonial period, underwent a significant doctrinal shift during Andrias III's reign. In 1695, a synod of Burawi bishops, with the approval of the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], issued a declaration condemning the slave trade as incompatible with Christian ethics. The declaration did not immediately free enslaved persons in Burawi territory, but it announced the Church's intention to phase out the institution and called upon the crown to cease granting licences for the trade.

The declaration caused outrage among the merchant class. The houses of [[House Guleed|Guleed]], [[House Warsame|Warsame]], and [[House Ciise|Ciise]], which dominated the slave trade from their bases in [[Mazar]] and [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]], viewed the Church's position as an existential threat to their economic interests. The merchants had previously been politically aligned with the [[Kadim-Galwa]] crown; now they began to reconsider their loyalties.

=== On the Dignity of All Peoples ===

{{main|On the Dignity of All Peoples}}

In 1700, a monastic scholar of the [[Order of Saint Guled|Order of ]] (a Burawi-specific monastic order) published a treatise titled '''"On the Dignity of All Peoples"''' (Burawi: ''Xogga Dhammaan Dadka''). The work argued, on theological and philosophical grounds, that all humans possessed an inherent dignity derived from their creation in the image of the divine, and that this dignity was violated by slavery, by extreme social hierarchy, and by the arbitrary exercise of power.

The treatise circulated widely among the literate classes — Church officials, royal administrators, merchant families, and the remaining Hoga aristocracy. It became a touchstone for the intellectual currents that would later be recognised as the Burawi precursor to the [[Enlightenment|era of enlightenment]]. The work was also controversial: the merchant houses viewed it as an attack on their economic interests, and traditionalists in the Church hierarchy considered it dangerously radical.

=== Orma-Jarin War ===

Burawa intervened in the [[Orma-Jarin War]] (1670s–1690s) on the side of [[Orma]], the highland kingdom that had long served as a buffer between Burawa and the eastern states. The intervention was a continuation of the historic policy of preventing any single power from dominating the region east of the Burawi highlands.

The war was inconclusive. Orma survived, but both Orma and [[Jarin]] were weakened by the prolonged conflict. The intervention consumed resources that might otherwise have been devoted to colonial defence or domestic development, and it demonstrated that Burawa's military capacity, while still formidable, was no longer sufficient to dictate outcomes in the eastern frontier.

=== Kaysoor-Mazar consolidation ===

While [[Kadim-Galwa]] held the throne, [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] and [[Kadim-Mazar]] quietly consolidated their economic power. The merchant houses of Mazar and Iskhal — particularly the Guleed, Warsame, and Ciise families — controlled the colonial trade networks and the financial system. Their wealth rivalled that of the crown, and their support was essential for any major state undertaking.

The Church's anti-slavery declaration accelerated the merchants' alienation from the Galwa crown. The Galwa dynasty, dependent on Church support for its legitimacy, could not simply defy the hierarchy. The merchants began to explore alternative political arrangements — including a potential restoration of [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] to the throne, with guarantees of merchant influence in government.

=== Sudden death ===

Andrias III died suddenly in 1709, without having designated an heir. The king's infertility had been known for years, but no succession plan had been prepared — a failure of statecraft that reflected either the king's unwillingness to confront his mortality or the inability of the court factions to agree on a solution.

The succession crisis was immediate. [[Kadim-Galwa]], through the Church's investigation of genealogical records, identified a great-nephew of Andrias III — a child named '''[[Cali IV]]''', descended from Nuur III through a collateral line — as the legitimate heir. [[Kadim-Kaysoor]], supported by the merchant houses and a coalition of Hoga families and military officers, rejected the claim as weak and advanced '''[[Iacob V]]''', an adult with his own power base and a more direct claim through the Kaysoor line.

== The 1709 Imperial Split ==

{{main|Burawa Imperial Split}}

The succession crisis of 1709 threatened to plunge the Burawi state into civil war. The two factions — Galwa backed by the Church, Kaysoor backed by merchants, Hogas, and elements of the military — mobilised their forces and prepared for conflict. The kingdom's external enemies, observing the crisis, began to consider interventions.

=== The factions ===

The '''Galwa faction''' was led by '''[[Sahra]]''', the mother of the child claimant Cali IV. Sahra was herself a member of the Galwa royal line: the granddaughter of Nuur III, cousin of Andrias III, and a woman of considerable political acumen. She assumed the regency for her son and worked to consolidate the Galwa position, drawing upon the support of the [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]], the western Hoga families, and military commanders loyal to the Galwa tradition.

The '''Kaysoor faction''' coalesced around '''[[Iacob V]]''', an adult claimant with his own resources and a power base in the eastern peninsula. The merchant houses of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] — Guleed, Warsame, and Ciise — provided financial support. The [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] families, who had been marginalised for a century and saw an opportunity to regain influence, offered military backing. Officers of the royal army, drawn primarily from the eastern provinces, declared for Iacob.

=== The four-house parliament ===

To secure the support of his heterogeneous coalition, Iacob V was compelled to make concessions. The most significant was the creation of a '''four-house parliament''' — a representative body that would share power with the crown. The four houses were:

1. The '''House of the King''', representing the royal authority
2. The '''House of the Church''', representing ecclesiastical interests (including those bishops and monasteries that aligned with Kaysoor)
3. The '''House of the Hogas''', granting the aristocracy a formal role in government for the first time
4. The '''House of the Landowners''', representing the merchant and planter elite

The parliament was a revolutionary innovation in Burawi governance, breaking centuries of absolutist tradition. It would become the foundation of representative government in the eastern kingdom.

=== The negotiated settlement ===

Both sides recognised that a civil war would invite foreign intervention. [[Volisania]], the historic rival, might support one faction in exchange for colonial concessions. [[Breisland]] and other Illypnian powers might exploit the chaos to expand their own positions in [[Abyala]]. The kingdom, already strained by decades of colonial competition, could not survive a protracted internal conflict.

Negotiations, mediated by the Church hierarchy — which had shifted from partisan support of Galwa to a neutral position seeking reconciliation — and the diplomatic corps, produced a settlement: the kingdom would be divided. '''[[Iacob V]]''' would rule the eastern portion, retaining the capital at [[Samara]], the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts, and the eastern peninsula. '''[[Cali IV]]''', with his mother '''[[Sahra]]''' as regent, would rule the western portion as the '''[[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]]''', with its capital at [[Hargiesa]].

The dividing line followed geographic boundaries: the [[Dimar River]] from its mouth to the [[Great Kaftian Lake]], then north along the lake to the [[Kitale River]], and thence to the sea. The division left both kingdoms viable, with sufficient agricultural land, population, and resources to function as independent states.

=== Colonial division ===

The colonial territories posed a particular challenge. Both factions claimed [[Burawi Cusub|New Burawa]], but the colonies were too distant to be easily controlled by either kingdom.

The settlement assigned '''[[Lacashe]]''' to the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]], but as an independent state under Burawi protection — a '''protectorate''' rather than a directly administered colony. '''[[Bindorama]]''' was assigned to the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] under the same arrangement. Both protectorates retained substantial autonomy in internal affairs while ceding defence and foreign policy to their respective patrons.

The '''[[Hesperian League]]''' (the former province of [[Arakwaya]]) rejected both arrangements. The League, which had functioned as a quasi-independent trading confederation since the early seventeenth century, declared full independence. Neither Burawa nor Galwa had the capacity to enforce its claim.

== Colonial governance ==

=== Administrative structure ===

The late colonial period saw the consolidation of a distinctive administrative structure in the Burawi colonial empire. The [[Burawi Cusub|New Burawa]] territories were governed through royal governors appointed in [[Samara]], supported by a civil bureaucracy of professional administrators. The [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts were military installations, governed by garrison commanders under the authority of the royal navy rather than the colonial office.

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated a parallel structure, with a colonial bishopric based in [[Lacashe]] and a network of parishes, missions, and monastic houses throughout the colonial sphere. The Church's role in colonial administration was substantial: it maintained records, provided education, and mediated disputes between settlers and indigenous communities.

=== Provincial divergence ===

The three provinces of New Burawa developed distinct trajectories during the late colonial period.

[[Lacashe]], the southernmost province, was the most firmly under Burawi control. The plantation economy, worked by enslaved [[Kaftia]]ns, produced sugar, tobacco, and cotton for export. The slave ports of Lacashe were among the busiest in the Abyalan trade. The provincial capital, also named Lacashe, was the seat of the colonial bishopric and the residence of the senior royal governor.

[[Bindorama]], the middle province, was a frontier zone. The mountainous terrain limited plantation agriculture; the economy was based on small-scale mining, mixed farming, and trade with the interior. The [[Dakari people|Dakari]] migrants who settled the province's valleys maintained distinct cultural traditions and a degree of autonomy unusual in the colonial context. Indigenous communities in Bindorama preserved more of their pre-conquest social organisation than in the south.

[[Arakwaya|The Hesperian League]] (former Arakwaya) was effectively independent by 1610 and remained so throughout the late colonial period. The League was a multi-ethnic trading confederation, with ships calling at [[New Samara]] from ports across the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] and [[Aethiopean Ocean|Aethiopean]] worlds. The League's gold rushes attracted fortune-seekers from every continent, and the central government in Samara exercised only nominal authority.

== Economy ==

=== Trade patterns ===

The late colonial economy was organised around the [[triangle trade]]: enslaved persons transported from [[Kaftia]] to [[Abyala]]; colonial goods — gold, sugar, tobacco, cotton — shipped from Abyala to the peninsula and to [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] markets; and manufactured goods and trade items exported from the peninsula to Kaftia. The [[War of the Mesogean]] and the loss of [[Reige]] had disrupted Burawi access to direct Mesogean trade, but the Atlantic routes remained highly profitable.

The merchant houses of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] — [[House Guleed|Guleed]], [[House Warsame|Warsame]], and [[House Ciise|Ciise]] — dominated the trade. Their fleets carried the majority of Burawi commercial traffic, and their financial networks extended throughout the colonial sphere. The wealth they accumulated rivalled that of the crown and the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy combined.

=== Slave trade ===

{{main|Kaftian slave trade}}

The [[Kaftian slave trade]] was a central component of the late colonial economy. Enslaved persons were captured or purchased in the [[Ayorin]] interior, the [[Tamazgha]] desert fringe, and the northern Kaftian coastlands, then transported to the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts or the peninsular ports for shipment to [[Abyala]].

The primary points of embarkation were [[Mazar]], the great Mesogean port; [[Xarunta Badda]], the North Kaftian naval base; and a port on the western [[Galwa]] coast that handled trade from the northern interior. By the late seventeenth century, an estimated 15,000 to 25,000 enslaved persons passed through these ports annually, bound for the plantation economies of Lacashe and the Volisanian colonies.

The Church's 1695 anti-slavery declaration marked the beginning of the trade's decline. The merchant houses fought the declaration, and the trade continued for years after the synod's pronouncement, but the moral and legal framework that had justified the institution was crumbling.

== Culture and intellectual life ==

=== Mesogean Renaissance influence ===

The [[Mesogean Renaissance]], the intellectual and artistic flowering that had begun in the Illypnian city-states two centuries earlier, reached Burawa in the late colonial period. Wealth from colonial trade funded the patronage of artists, architects, and scholars. [[Samara]] and [[Mazar]] became centres of cultural production, with workshops producing paintings, sculptures, and decorative arts that blended Mesogean forms with Kaftian traditions.

Illypnian artisans traveled to Burawa, attracted by royal and mercantile patronage. They brought with them the techniques and aesthetic principles of the Renaissance — perspective in painting, classical proportion in architecture, humanist themes in literature. Burawi artists adapted these influences to local materials and sensibilities, producing a distinctive [[Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis]].

=== Intellectual centres ===

Intellectual life flourished in multiple centres. The royal court at Samara sponsored historians, philosophers, and scientists. The [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] maintained monastic schools and universities where theology, natural philosophy, and the classics were studied. The merchant class, increasingly literate and cosmopolitan, established libraries, salons, and informal networks for the exchange of ideas.

The intellectual ferment was not confined to the elite. The [[gewel]] bardic tradition, though marginalised, survived in the northern territories, preserving oral histories and poetic forms that predated the Christian era. In [[Bindorama]], the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] migrants maintained their own cultural practices, blending northern Kaftian traditions with the indigenous influences of the Abyalan frontier.

=== The Great Testimony ===

{{main|Great Testimony}}

The '''[[Great Testimony]]''' (1685) was the most ambitious intellectual project of the period. Commissioned by Faarax IV, it compiled the history of Burawa from the founding of the kingdom in 403 AD to the late seventeenth century. A team of chroniclers, Church scholars, and [[gewel]] bards worked for a decade to synthesise written records, archival documents, and oral traditions.

The Testimony served multiple purposes. It was a legitimising chronicle of the [[Kadim]] dynasty, documenting the continuity of the royal line and the divine favour that had enabled Burawi expansion. It was an attempt to preserve traditions that were being lost — the gewel oral histories, the legends of the pre-Christian era, the memory of the [[Ufyen|Ufyen kingdom]] that had once ruled the highlands. And it was a work of scholarship, applying the critical methods of the Mesogean humanists to the Burawi past.

=== On the Dignity of All Peoples ===

{{main|On the Dignity of All Peoples}}

The treatise '''"On the Dignity of All Peoples"''' (1700) was the most radical intellectual work of the late colonial period. Written by a monastic scholar of a Burawi-specific order, the work argued that all humans possessed inherent dignity by virtue of their creation in the divine image. From this premise, the author derived a critique of slavery, of extreme social hierarchy, and of arbitrary power.

The treatise circulated in manuscript form among the literate elite. It was praised by reformers and condemned by traditionalists; the merchant houses viewed it as an attack on their economic interests, while the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy saw in it a challenge to the entire social order. The work's influence extended beyond the late colonial period, becoming a foundational text for the Burawi strand of enlightenment thought.

== Military ==

=== Naval technology ===

The [[War of the Mesogean]] had demonstrated the obsolescence of the Burawi fleet. The ships built for coastal defence and colonial logistics were outclassed by the modernised Volisanian navy. The loss of 50 vessels at the [[Battle of Reige]] was a catastrophe from which the fleet never fully recovered.

Under Faarax IV, the royal shipyards at [[Badalga]] developed the [[Badalga-class]] vessel designed for the Atlantic route. The new ships were longer, faster, and more manoeuvrable than their predecessors, with improved cargo capacity and enhanced armament. They were not warships of the line — Burawa had abandoned any ambition to contest the [[Mesogean Sea]] — but they were well-suited for escorting convoys, suppressing piracy, and projecting power along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast.

=== Orma-Jarin War ===

Burawi intervention in the [[Orma-Jarin War]] (1670s–1690s) demonstrated both the kingdom's continued military capacity and its limitations. The intervention was limited in scope: financial support, military advisors, and a contingent of royal troops. Burawa could not commit the full strength of its army to a peripheral conflict without leaving the peninsula and the colonies vulnerable.

The war was costly and inconclusive. [[Orma]] survived as an independent state, but the eastern frontier remained unstable. The intervention confirmed that Burawa's strategic priorities lay elsewhere — in the colonial sphere, where the wealth of the kingdom was generated, rather than in the eastern highlands.

=== Volisania relations ===

The [[Aethiopean War]] of the 1660s was a brief naval war with the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], triggered by Volisanian seizure of Burawi sailors for service in Volisania's colonial conflicts. The war was fought primarily in [[Abyala]]n waters and along the trade routes. Neither side achieved a decisive advantage, and the conflict ended with a negotiated settlement that reaffirmed Burawi neutrality.

By the 1680s, relations had improved. The [[Abyalan Anti-Piracy Campaign]] against indigenous pirates in Abyala demonstrated that the two powers could cooperate when their interests aligned. The rapprochement would prove crucial during the succession crisis of 1709, when the threat of Volisanian intervention helped motivate the negotiated settlement.

== Christianity and the Church ==

=== Church-Galwa alliance ===

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] had been a pillar of [[Kadim-Galwa]] legitimacy since the branch's accession in 1608. The Church had supported the Galwa claim against [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] after the [[War of the Mesogean]], and the Galwa kings had rewarded the Church with patronage, privileges, and influence in state affairs.

The alliance was mutually beneficial. The Galwa dynasty relied upon the Church for legitimacy, particularly during succession transitions. The Church relied upon the crown for protection, patronage, and the enforcement of ecclesiastical authority. The relationship was not without tension — the crown periodically chafed at Church influence, and reformers within the Church criticised royal policies — but the alliance endured throughout the late colonial period.

=== Anti-slavery position ===

The Church's 1695 declaration against slavery was a watershed. For nearly two centuries, the Church had accommodated the institution, baptising enslaved persons during the Middle Passage and operating parishes for enslaved populations in the colonies. The theological justification had been that enslavement facilitated the conversion of non-Christian peoples.

The 1695 synod reversed this position. Drawing upon the intellectual currents of the [[Mesogean Renaissance]] and the arguments of reformers within the Burawi Church, the bishops declared that the slave trade was incompatible with Christian ethics. The declaration did not immediately emancipate enslaved persons, but it announced the Church's intention to phase out the institution and called upon the crown to cease licensing the trade.

The declaration alienated the merchant class, which viewed it as a threat to their economic foundation. It also strained the Church-Galwa alliance, as the crown was caught between its ecclesiastical ally and its financial base. The tension contributed to the merchant houses' openness to a [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] restoration.


=== Role in succession ===

The Church played a decisive role in the succession crisis of 1709. The [[Cardinal of Burawa]] and the episcopal hierarchy initially recognised the Galwa claim — the child '''[[Cali IV]]''', great-nephew of Andrias III — and provided the theological justification for the succession. The Church's support gave the Galwa faction legitimacy and seemed to presage a schism, with the possibility that the Kaysoor faction might be forced to break with the Burawi hierarchy.

As the crisis deepened, however, the Church's position evolved. The Cardinal and his advisors recognised that a civil war would devastate the faithful on both sides and might lead to a permanent rupture in the Burawi Catholic community. The precedent of the [[War of the Mesogean]] — when the sight of co-religionists warring against each other had scandalised the Catholic world — weighed heavily on the hierarchy's deliberations.

The Church ultimately chose reconciliation over division. In the negotiations that produced the settlement, the Church agreed to recognise '''[[Iacob V]]''' as the legitimate king of the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]], provided that the kingdom remained Catholic and that the Church retained its traditional privileges. In exchange, the Kaysoor faction agreed that the Church would not be penalised for its initial support of Galwa, and that ecclesiastical properties in the east would remain under Church control.

The compromise preserved the unity of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. The Church emerged from the crisis not as the partisan of one faction, but as a supra-national institution that maintained spiritual authority in both kingdoms. The Cardinal of Burawa, whose seat was formally transferred from [[Samara]] to [[Weli Markos]] — the city that had grown around the great cathedral built under Faarax IV and had served as the de facto religious centre for half a century — as part of the reconciliation, retained his position as head of the Church in both states. Bishops in the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] remained in communion with the Cardinal and through him with the broader Catholic world.

The [[Bartay Society]] was a fraternal religious order founded in 1611 with the approval of the head of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. Modelled in part on the institutional methods of the Volisanian Jesuits, the society was dedicated to evangelisation, education, and charity. Over time, it developed its own organisational apparatus across [[Burawa]], [[Galwa]], [[Bindorama]], and [[Lacashe]], establishing schools and charitable institutions that extended the Church's social influence.

The Bartay Society remained closely tied to ecclesiastical authority during the late colonial period, but its educational work also exposed Burawi students to broader intellectual currents. In later decades, some of its schools became associated with more liberal teaching methods, particularly in urban areas and colonial centres. The society would continue to play an important role in Burawi education long after the end of the colonial era.

This arrangement gave the Church a unique role in the post-split era. As the only institution that spanned the divided kingdom, the Church became a natural intermediary between [[Samara]] and [[Hargiesa]], operating from the neutral ground of Weli Markos, facilitating communication, mediating disputes, and preserving a sense of shared Burawi Catholic identity across the political divide. The Church's role as liaison would prove essential in the decades that followed, as the two kingdoms navigated their complex relationship.
